WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.121
The core of this ethical blueprint is the
concept of the three R's,

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.901
and the most critical directive among them
is replacement.

3
00:00:07.621 --> 00:00:10.280
This animated flowchart illustrates the
primary rule.

4
00:00:10.881 --> 00:00:14.440
If it is possible to answer a scientific
question without utilizing an animal,

5
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.280
the experimental design must bypass the
living subject entirely.

6
00:00:18.881 --> 00:00:23.440
Researchers are required to utilize recognized
non-animal methodologies whenever feasible,

7
00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:28.640
specifically prioritizing advanced in vitro
cell cultures and in silico computer modeling.

8
00:00:28.881 --> 00:00:32.800
When an animal model is deemed
scientifically unavoidable, a secondary

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.200
rule takes effect, phylogenetic
substitution.

10
00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:41.640
This dictates that researchers must attempt to replace
evolutionarily higher animals with those lower

11
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.901
on the phylogenetic tree.

12
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.940
If the data can be gathered using a lower
species,

13
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.261
the higher species model is rejected.

14
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.821
Replacement serves as the initial,
rigorous filter that every single

15
00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:56.821
experimental protocol must pass before any
live subject is acquired.

16
00:00:57.481 --> 00:01:00.800
Beyond the mechanics of the three R's,
daily laboratory conduct

17
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.521
is governed by four distinct ethical
responsibilities.
